<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Kassite Babylonia</h1><p class="st-meta">1595 BCE – 1155 BCE (approx.) · capital Babylon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The longest-ruling dynasty in Babylonian history: Kassite kings from the Zagros who reunited Babylonia and made it a great power of the Amarna age.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a>, a people from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> with their own language and gods, had settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in the 18th century BCE. After the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mursili-i">Mursili I</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 1595 BCE, Kassite rulers took over the city, perhaps first under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agum-ii">Agum II</a>, and around 1460 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulamburiash">Ulamburiash</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sealand-dynasty">Sealand dynasty</a>, uniting all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, called Karduniash.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Kassite kings adopted Babylonian language, religion and court culture, promoting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurigalzu-i">Kurigalzu I</a> built a new royal city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dur-kurigalzu">Dur-Kurigalzu</a>, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a>. In the 14th century kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadashman-enlil-i">Kadashman-Enlil I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burna-buriash-ii">Burna-Buriash II</a> exchanged letters, gold and princesses with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> correspondence. Land grants were recorded on carved boundary stones (kudurrus), horses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> were prized, and scribes standardised literary works such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukulti-ninurta-i">Tukulti-Ninurta I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> around 1225 BCE. In 1158 BCE the Elamite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shutruk-nahhunte">Shutruk-Nahhunte</a> invaded, and around 1155 BCE the last Kassite king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil-nadin-ahi">Enlil-nadin-ahi</a>, was carried off, after which the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-dynasty-of-isin">Second Dynasty of Isin</a> took power.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kassite-babylonia.jpg" alt="Kassite Babylonia" width="320" height="281"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kassite_Babylonia_EN.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1595 BCE – 1155 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Babylon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kurigalzu I, Burna-Buriash II, Nazi-Maruttash</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Babylonian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akkadian, Kassite</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kassite dynasty, Karduniash, Third Dynasty of Babylon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1375&amp;focus=kassite-babylonia" aria-label="Kassite Babylonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5810 967 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kassite Babylonia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="